# Can a full hotel take one more guest? Hilbert's Hotel and Cantor's diagonal
tags: ["infinity","countable sets","set theory"]
teaches: ["concepts/countable-infinity","methods/diagonal-argument","concepts/sizes-of-infinity"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed","b":"gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace","take":"lines"}
prompt: "How can a full infinite hotel make room for a busload of new guests? Why can't any list contain every decimal number? What does it mean for one infinity to be bigger than another?"
next: {"title":"Can one ball become two? The Banach–Tarski paradox","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/paradoxes/03-can-one-ball-become-two-the-banach.cast"}

## Hilbert's Hotel and Cantor's diagonal
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Cantor would have given you a room.
    good: Infinity bends a few rules. You bent with it.
    poor: Infinity fools everyone the first time. Cantor's critics included.
    none: No answers? The hotel still has room for you.
sources:
    - id: kragh2014
      title: The True (?) Story of Hilbert's Infinite Hotel
      authors: Helge Kragh
      year: 2014
      finding: Hilbert introduced the infinite hotel in an unpublished lecture of January 1924; George Gamow's One Two Three … Infinity (1947) made it widely known.
      url: https://arxiv.org/abs/1403.0059
    - id: cantor1891
      title: Über eine elementare Frage der Mannigfaltigkeitslehre
      authors: Georg Cantor
      year: 1891
      finding: 'The diagonal argument: no list of endless two-symbol sequences contains them all; every set has more subsets than members (Jahresbericht der DMV 1: 75–78).'
    - id: cantor1874
      title: Über eine Eigenschaft des Inbegriffes aller reellen algebraischen Zahlen
      authors: Georg Cantor
      year: 1874
      finding: 'The algebraic numbers, fractions among them, can be listed; the real numbers cannot (J. reine angew. Math. 77: 258–262).'
    - id: godel1940
      title: The Consistency of the Continuum Hypothesis
      authors: Kurt Gödel
      year: 1940
      finding: The continuum hypothesis cannot be disproved from the usual axioms of set theory.
    - id: cohen1963
      title: The independence of the continuum hypothesis
      authors: Paul J. Cohen
      year: 1963
      finding: 'The continuum hypothesis cannot be proved from the usual axioms of set theory (Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. 50: 1143–1148).'
    - id: hilbert1926
      title: Über das Unendliche
      authors: David Hilbert
      year: 1926
      finding: '"Aus dem Paradies, das Cantor uns geschaffen, soll uns niemand vertreiben können" (Mathematische Annalen 95: 161–190).'

