    card title "Can a full hotel take one more guest?"

A: Here's a hotel with a room for every whol (@draw r1 n1@)e number (@draw r2 n2@): room one (@draw r3 n3 r4 n4 r5 n5 r6 n6@), two, three,  (@draw dots@)and on fo (@draw guests parallel true@)rever. Tonight every room is taken.

B: Then the sign says no vacancies. A new guest t (@draw gn gn_l@)urns up, and I send her down the road.
    draw full

A: In an ordinary hotel, yes. Here the (@draw rule1@) manager asks every guest to move up (@move g1 g2 g3 g4 g5 g6 by [140,0] duration 2.2@) one: room one to two, two to three, and so on.

B: And the guest in the last room?
    point at.ref dots gesture circle

A: There is no last room. Every guest has a next room to go to, a (@highlight r1@)nd room one is empty.

    erase g6 gn_l

B: So a full hotel took one more, and nobody sleeps in the corridor.
    move gn to.x 90 to.y 425 duration 1.2

    pause 0.4

A: David Hilbert told this story in a lecture in  (@draw h_1924@)1924, and George Gamow made it famou (@draw h_1947@)s in a book in 1947.
    draw hilbert

    erase hilbert h_1924 h_1947

A: Here's our route: fit in a busload, meet a crowd that won't fit, and see what bigger means when everything is infinite.
    blocking false

    highlight guests gn duration 2.5

    highlight dots duration 3

B: A busload. How big a bus?
    draw bus

A: Infinitel (@draw bus_inf@)y many seats: seat one, two, three, forever. Moving up o (@highlight rule1@)ne room frees only one room.

    erase rule1

A: So the manager needs a new rule. Where should the guest in room n go?
    draw rule2

    ask "Which rule empties infinitely many rooms? Pick the tile." on rule2 others ["n + 1","n + 100"] store rule voice a
        right: Two n: everyone doubles, and every odd room empties.
        wrong: The new rule must free more than one room, or a hundred.

    erase rule2_tiles

A: Room two n. One goe (@move gn to.x 230 to.y 425 duration 1@)s to two, two  (@move g1 to.x 510 to.y 425 duration 1@)to four, three (@move g2 to.x 790 to.y 425 duration 1@) to six, and every gu (@move g3 g4 g5 to.x 930 to.y 425 duration 1.2@)est lands in an even room.

    erase g3 g4 g5

B: Which leav (@highlight r1 r3 r5@)es every odd room empty: one, three, five, forever. Exactly (@draw b1 b3 b5 parallel true@) one for each seat on the bus.

A: That's the first cr (@draw full_x@)ack. Full means no room only when the rooms run out, and  (@highlight dots@)here they never do.

    pause 0.5

B: Then infinity swallows everything. Any crowd, however huge, fits somewhere.
    highlight rooms

    clear

    card title "A crowd that won't fit"

A: Let's test that with the hardest crowd. (@draw L1 L2 L3 L4 L5@) Every guest on this bus wears a number between zero and one, written as an endless decimal.

B: Like a half, o (@draw row4@)r a third?
    draw row1

A: Or pi (@draw row2@) without its three, or e w (@draw row3@)ithout its two, or root (@draw row5@) two without its one. Any endless decimal at all.

B: Say the manager claims his list has them all, room by room.
    highlight L1 L2 L3 L4 L5

A: Georg Cantor showed how to catch him out. Read down the diagonal: the f (@draw c1@)irst digit of room one (@draw c2@), the second of ro (@draw c3 c4 c5 parallel true@)om two, and so on.
    draw cantor

    erase cantor

A: (@highlight d11 d22 d33 d44 d55@) Five, four, eight, three, one. Build a new number  (@draw plus1@)by changing each of them: add one.

B: And an eight or a nine?
    highlight d33

A: Those become  (@draw plus2@)a one. That keeps us clear of endless nines: zero point  (@draw nines@)four nine nine nine, forever, is just a half in disguise.

    erase nines

B: So the new number starts with a six, then a five.
    draw new_l nn

    ask "Room three's diagonal digit is 8. Which digit comes next in the new number?" on nn others ["9","8"] store dig voice b
        right: One: an eight becomes a one.
        wrong: Check what the rule does to an eight.

    erase nn nn_tiles

A: (@draw nn2@) A one, then four, then two: zero point six five one four two, and on down the endless list.

B: Fine. So which room is it in?
    draw roomq

A: Not room one: they differ in the first digit. Not room two: they differ in the second.
    blocking false

    highlight d11 effect circle duration 2.4

    highlight M1 effect circle duration 2.4

    highlight d22 effect circle duration 2.4

    highlight M2 effect circle duration 2.4

    ask "Could the new number be in room four? Tap the digit that rules it out." choose ["d41","d42","d43","d44","d45"] answer d44 store r4 voice b
        right: The fourth: room four has a 3 there, the new number a 4.
        wrong: Room four's number is checked at its fourth digit.

A: And so on for every room: it differs from room n in digit  (@draw nolist@)n. It's on nobody's list.

    erase r4_ring roomq

B: Then squeeze it in. Move (@move rd1 rd2 rd3 rd4 by [0,-90] duration 1.6@) everyone up one, l (@draw nrow parallel true@)ike before.
    erase diag nolist rd5

A: He can. But the new list has a ne (@draw q1@)w diagonal, and t (@draw q2 q3 q4 q5 parallel true@)hat builds another missing number. Ever (@highlight diag2@)y list misses someone.

B: So the hotel isn't too small. These guests can't be put in a line at all.
    draw nn_box

    pause 0.5

    clear

    card title "Sizes of infinity"

A: That's what bigger means (@draw toprow@) here. Two crowds are the same size  (@draw botrow@)if you can pair them off one to one, with nobody left over.

B: Which is why the even numbers count as many as all the whole numbers. Odd, but fair.
    draw pairs

    erase toprow botrow pairs

A: And the decimals are more: every pairing with the  (@draw crowds@)rooms leaves someone out. A crowd that fits the hotel is called countable.

    ask "Which crowds fit Hilbert's Hotel, and which are too many? Sort each card." on crowds store s voice b
        right: {s} of {s.total} on the first try.
        wrong: {s} of {s.total} on the first try.

A: Fractions are the surprise. Cantor showed in 1874 that they can be listed too, so they fit.
    highlight crowds_3

B: So of that lot, only the decimals overflow. Even the ones between zero and a tenth would.
    highlight crowds_2

    clear

    card title "A tower of infinities"

A: Nor does it st (@draw k1@)op at two sizes. The same di (@draw ka1 kbig1 k2@)agonal trick shows that the ways o (@draw ka2 kbig2 k3 parallel true@)f picking some of a set's members always outnumber the members.

B: So above every infinity sits a b (@draw ktop@)igger one. A tower with no top.

B: Is there anything in between the whole numbers and the decimals?
    draw kq

A: (@highlight kq@) Cantor guessed not, and never proved it. Gödel in 1940 and Paul Cohen in 1963 (@draw unsettled@) showed the usual axioms can't settle it either way.

A: Cantor's infinities met fierce resistance. Hilbert's ans (@draw quote@)wer, in translation: no one shall expel us from the paradise tha (@draw quote_t@)t Cantor has created.

B: So the step that broke was "infinite is infinite".
    highlight k1 k2

A: Yes: an infinite hotel always has room for one more, or a busload more, (@highlight tower@) but some crowds are too big for any list, and the diagonal proves it.

    quiz "Guests arrive, each holding an endless list of coin tosses: heads, tails, tails, heads… Can the full hotel give every one a room?" voice b
        right: No, and not for lack of variety: line them up in rooms, flip every toss on the diagonal, and you get a sequence nobody holds. Cantor's own 1891 proof used exactly such two-symbol strings.
        wrong: Suppose they all got rooms, and read down the diagonal.
        * Yes: move everyone up
        * No: only heads and tails
        + No: flip the diagonal

    clear

    card title "Can a full hotel take one more guest?"

B: So: can a full hotel take one more guest? (@draw yes@) Yes. J (@draw zout zout_l noteveryone@)ust not every guest.
    draw zrooms

