audio:
  lang: en
  lines:
    '|a||Here''s a hotel with a room for every whole number: room one, two, three, and on forever. Tonight every room is taken.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7520
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|b||Then the sign says no vacancies. A new guest turns up, and I send her down the road.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5680
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:12n5pid'
    '|a||In an ordinary hotel, yes. Here the manager asks every guest to move up one: room one to two, two to three, and so on.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9040
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:kqzm5d'
    '|b||And the guest in the last room?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 2160
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:gl6vqu'
    '|a||There is no last room. Every guest has a next room to go to, and room one is empty.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6520
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1kldf6k'
    '|b||So a full hotel took one more, and nobody sleeps in the corridor.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4960
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:1vyoggh'
    '|a||David Hilbert told this story in a lecture in 1924, and George Gamow made it famous in a book in 1947.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7400
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1q29psu'
    '|a||Here''s our route: fit in a busload, meet a crowd that won''t fit, and see what bigger means when everything is infinite.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7880
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:4092ci'
    '|b||A busload. How big a bus?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 2840
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:ri1not'
    '|a||Infinitely many seats: seat one, two, three, forever. Moving up one room frees only one room.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6080
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1d3kr1f'
    '|a||So the manager needs a new rule. Where should the guest in room n go?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5680
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:o3c8fa'
    '|a||Which rule empties infinitely many rooms? Pick the tile.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4000
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:6dmgfa'
    '|a||Room two n. One goes to two, two to four, three to six, and every guest lands in an even room.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7600
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|b||Which leaves every odd room empty: one, three, five, forever. Exactly one for each seat on the bus.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8160
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:d1ip19'
    '|a||That''s the first crack. Full means no room only when the rooms run out, and here they never do.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6520
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1gb3no9'
    '|b||Then infinity swallows everything. Any crowd, however huge, fits somewhere.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6200
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:edgsxp'
    '|a||Let''s test that with the hardest crowd. Every guest on this bus wears a number between zero and one, written as an endless decimal.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8440
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:uvnkrd'
    '|b||Like a half, or a third?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 2520
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:12rbefz'
    '|a||Or pi without its three, or e without its two, or root two without its one. Any endless decimal at all.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8040
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:7vppwh'
    '|b||Say the manager claims his list has them all, room by room.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3560
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:16p8xow'
    '|a||Georg Cantor showed how to catch him out. Read down the diagonal: the first digit of room one, the second of room two, and so on.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8040
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1kakyne'
    '|a||Five, four, eight, three, one. Build a new number by changing each of them: add one.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6600
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:apwpvu'
    '|b||And an eight or a nine?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 1800
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:5tdeui'
    '|a||Those become a one. That keeps us clear of endless nines: zero point four nine nine nine, forever, is just a half in disguise.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8840
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1s2nqq4'
    '|b||So the new number starts with a six, then a five.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3400
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:yoo6b0'
    '|b||Room three''s diagonal digit is 8. Which digit comes next in the new number?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5400
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:uiy0n0'
    '|a||A one, then four, then two: zero point six five one four two, and on down the endless list.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8400
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|b||Fine. So which room is it in?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 2280
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:1oz7kx6'
    '|a||Not room one: they differ in the first digit. Not room two: they differ in the second.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5760
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1sxloc3'
    '|b||Could the new number be in room four? Tap the digit that rules it out.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4120
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:1peky63'
    '|b||The fourth: room four has a 3 there, the new number a 4.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4440
      voice: gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace
    '|a||And so on for every room: it differs from room n in digit n. It''s on nobody''s list.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6400
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|b||Then squeeze it in. Move everyone up one, like before.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3200
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:126yarh'
    '|a||He can. But the new list has a new diagonal, and that builds another missing number. Every list misses someone.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7880
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1de8b38'
    '|b||So the hotel isn''t too small. These guests can''t be put in a line at all.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4520
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:1ln6jxx'
    '|a||That''s what bigger means here. Two crowds are the same size if you can pair them off one to one, with nobody left over.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7120
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:zkru60'
    '|b||Which is why the even numbers count as many as all the whole numbers. Odd, but fair.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5800
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:1akcrkz'
    '|a||And the decimals are more: every pairing with the rooms leaves someone out. A crowd that fits the hotel is called countable.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7840
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:hjyl2h'
    '|b||Which crowds fit Hilbert''s Hotel, and which are too many? Sort each card.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5840
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:vmbqw3'
    '|a||Fractions are the surprise. Cantor showed in 1874 that they can be listed too, so they fit.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6800
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|b||So of that lot, only the decimals overflow. Even the ones between zero and a tenth would.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5600
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:1v5eyx5'
    '|a||Nor does it stop at two sizes. The same diagonal trick shows that the ways of picking some of a set''s members always outnumber the members.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9000
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1438dik'
    '|b||So above every infinity sits a bigger one. A tower with no top.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5000
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:12nmwus'
    '|b||Is there anything in between the whole numbers and the decimals?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3280
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:31wuhu'
    '|a||Cantor guessed not, and never proved it. Gödel in 1940 and Paul Cohen in 1963 showed the usual axioms can''t settle it either way.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10840
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1s0vogc'
    '|a||Cantor''s infinities met fierce resistance. Hilbert''s answer, in translation: no one shall expel us from the paradise that Cantor has created.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9800
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1ar9viq'
    '|b||So the step that broke was "infinite is infinite".':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3400
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:6ya5rb'
    '|a||Yes: an infinite hotel always has room for one more, or a busload more, but some crowds are too big for any list, and the diagonal proves it.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10360
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1911gt2'
    '|b||Guests arrive, each holding an endless list of coin tosses: heads, tails, tails, heads… Can the full hotel give every one a room?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7880
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:1sic9fi'
    '|b||No, and not for lack of variety: line them up in rooms, flip every toss on the diagonal, and you get a sequence nobody holds. Cantor''s own 1891 proof used exactly such two-symbol strings.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11880
      voice: gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace
    '|b||So: can a full hotel take one more guest? Yes. Just not every guest.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5600
      voice: gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace
    '|a||Next: Can one ball become two? The Banach–Tarski paradox':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5640
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|a||Two n: everyone doubles, and every odd room empties.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4400
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|a||The new rule must free more than one room, or a hundred.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3840
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|a||Cantor would have given you a room.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 1880
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|a||Infinity bends a few rules. You bent with it.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3560
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|a||Infinity fools everyone the first time. Cantor''s critics included.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4640
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|a||No answers? The hotel still has room for you.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3080
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|b||One: an eight becomes a one.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3440
      voice: gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace
    '|b||Check what the rule does to an eight.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 2400
      voice: gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace
    '|b||Cantor would have given you a room.':
